nsclient for Debian
===================

Configuration file permissions
-------------------------------

The main configuration file /etc/nsclient/nsclient.ini may contain
credentials (NRPE/NSCA/check_mk passwords, allowed-hosts secrets, ...),
and so may any file it includes. To keep them from being world-readable
while still letting the daemon read them, the package sets:

    /etc/nsclient               root:nsclient  0750
    /etc/nsclient/nsclient.ini  root:nsclient  0640

(root owns them; the "nsclient" service group, under which the daemon
runs, gets read access.) The directory mode also covers files you add
there yourself, such as included configuration files, whatever their own
mode. These permissions are applied by the postinst on every configuration
run.

The daemon cannot write its own configuration: settings changes are made
as root (e.g. `sudo nscp settings ...`).

Customising the permissions
---------------------------

A plain `chmod`/`chown` on the directory or the file is NOT persistent: it
is reset on the next package upgrade. To change the owner, group or mode
permanently, register a dpkg-statoverride - the postinst then leaves that
path alone:

    # e.g. restrict the file to the service user only (the daemon runs as
    # nsclient, and root can still edit it regardless of the mode):
    dpkg-statoverride --update --add nsclient nsclient 0600 /etc/nsclient/nsclient.ini

Note that the daemon runs with group "nsclient", so any override you set
must still let that user/group reach the directory and read the file,
otherwise the service will fail to start.

To go back to the package-managed permissions, remove the override:

    dpkg-statoverride --remove /etc/nsclient/nsclient.ini

Editing the file
----------------

Edit it as root (e.g. `sudoedit /etc/nsclient/nsclient.ini`). It is a
dpkg conffile, so your changes are preserved across upgrades.

